  [1mNext fork: what's allowed to leave the browser.[0m This one matters more than usual here. The current [38;5;153mapp.js[39m already does
  [38;5;153mconsole.log("Logging in:", username)[39m inside the login handler, and the plan is now to ship browser logs to a
  third-party vendor. Anything the logger picks up automatically — form state, unhandled exception payloads, network
  request bodies — could carry a password out of the user's browser to someone else's servers. That's a data-exposure
  consequence, so I want your call on the policy rather than picking a default.

  - [1mAllowlist (default-deny)[0m — only fields the logger is explicitly told to include get sent; everything else is dropped
    before the record is built. A password can't leak through a field nobody thought about, including fields added
    months from now. Costs you thinner logs and a bit of friction each time you want a new field. Safe by construction.
  - [1mRedaction denylist (default-allow)[0m — build the full record, then scrub known-sensitive keys ([38;5;153mpassword[39m, [38;5;153mtoken[39m,
    [38;5;153mauthorization[39m, etc). Richer logs with less upkeep, but it fails open: any new sensitive field leaks silently until
    someone remembers to add it to the list. That's the failure mode that produces credentials-in-logs incidents.
  - [1mAllowlist plus opt-in redaction for free-text[0m — allowlist for structured fields, and a scrubber over message strings
    and stack traces, which are the places secrets sneak in even under an allowlist.

  Cheap to change later: adding fields to an allowlist. Expensive: discovering a year of vendor-side logs contains
  passwords — you can't unsend that, and vendor retention means deletion is a support ticket, not a code change.

  My recommendation is the third option. The allowlist gives you the default-deny guarantee, and the string scrubber
  covers the gap an allowlist alone leaves, since [38;5;153merror.message[39m and stack traces are free-text that an allowlist waves
  through.
